---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import MonoLabel from '../components/MonoLabel.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import portrait from '../assets/photos/about-portrait.jpg';
import barcelona from '../assets/photos/barcelona.jpg';
import chess from '../assets/photos/chess.jpg';
import '../styles/prose.css';
---
<BaseLayout title="About" description="How Fortune Max-Eguakun got into building software, from PvP games in a school computer lab to running engineering at a VC platform.">
  <section class="tone-dark page">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="top">
        <div>
          <MonoLabel text="About" />
          <h1 class="display title">From a school computer lab to a platform investors use.</h1>
          <div class="prose intro">
            <p>
            It started when I was 14, with small PvP games for my friends. They'd suggest characters, I'd make them,
            and they'd use them as their avatars when we fought. From there I moved on to
            modding GTA San Andreas, changing the gameplay, the maps and how things behaved.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <Image class="portrait" src={portrait} alt="Fortune standing in a doorway, in a white T-shirt" widths={[320, 640]} sizes="(max-width: 820px) 70vw, 320px" loading="eager" />
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A few months later I decided to learn it properly, starting with Java for Android apps and Python for scripting. Wanting to see what was underneath Android took me into Linux and rooting phones, and C++ came with it: first for programs I could run on the phone itself, later for competitive programming.
        </p>
        <p>
          The biggest thing I built in that phase started as a memory for my own phone: using root access, it captured my screen every 30 seconds and saved it to my Google Drive, so if I'd seen something and forgotten where, I could search for it. It grew into an anti-theft tool that survives a factory reset: it hooks into a system service that starts on every boot, gives me remote access over SSH or Telnet, and runs a script I'd set up in advance, to report the phone's location, lock it or remove a password.
        </p>

        <h2>Engineering, then software</h2>
        <figure class="side">
          <Image src={chess} alt="Fortune smiling over a chess board at night, with the moon over the sea behind him" widths={[300, 600]} sizes="(max-width: 820px) 100vw, 300px" />
          <figcaption>Chess by the sea.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>
          I did my bachelor's in mechanical engineering at the University of Benin. I already liked programming and
          figured an engineering degree would teach me things I wouldn't pick up on my own. My final-year project was a
          hybrid go-kart with an engine and an electric motor. I enjoy physical engineering, but I like software more,
          because you can go from an idea to something people use much faster.
        </p>
        <p>
          Competitive programming filled the gaps: Code Jam, Hash Code, Hacker Cup, and TON smart-contract contests. I
          regularly placed near the top in Nigeria. Mostly I just liked how the problems made me think, and I'd sometimes spend
          longer on one than I should have.
        </p>

        <h2>Bank of America, and a Raspberry Pi</h2>
        <p>
          In 2019 I had internship offers from Goldman Sachs and Bank of America. I used the Goldman offer to ask Bank of America for a longer placement, and took theirs: six months in Dublin instead of a summer. It was my first time on a big team, and I learned a lot
          about reporting on my work and asking good questions. I helped with the move from Python 2 to 3 before Python
          2's end of life and built a dashboard for viewing trades from different booking systems, working with a team across London and India.
        </p>
        <p>
          Around the same time my university asked me to look at how they managed student results. It was an MS Access
          database. I proposed something better and built the first version with two other students: registration, results, GPA, and the result sheets that get printed and pinned up each semester. It's sensitive work, because a GPA decides whether someone graduates. It first ran on a Raspberry Pi on campus, because the school wanted it on their premises. In 2024 I rebuilt it on my own, and today it runs in the cloud for a few cents per department a month, with the frontend on a free tier, so the only real bill is the domain. Five departments use it, and I'm still shipping updates.
        </p>

        <h2>Master's, and OneRagtime</h2>
        <figure class="banner">
          <Image src={barcelona} alt="Fortune on a rooftop in Barcelona, with the Sagrada Família behind him" widths={[720, 1440]} sizes="(max-width: 820px) 100vw, 720px" />
          <figcaption>Barcelona, where I've lived since starting my master's.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>
          For my master's at Harbour.Space in Barcelona I majored in front-end engineering, because it was the side I knew least: I'd always built backends and kept the interfaces simple. The degree showed me how much engineering goes into an interface that feels fast, from how modern frameworks render and manage state to moving heavy work off the main thread with web workers, WebAssembly and canvas. I also took a minor in data science, which fed straight into the data and LLM work I was doing at OneRagtime. My capstone was <a href="/work/cloudstation3">CloudStation3</a>: I couldn't bring my PlayStation to Barcelona, so I built a way to stream my PS3 games to any device with a browser.
        </p>
        <p>
          During the master's, in April 2023, I joined OneRagtime, a VC platform, as an intern, and at the end of the year they offered me a full-time role. There were six engineers and a designer on the tech team then. People left one at a time, and by mid-2025 we were two, with me owning the architecture and the technical decisions. Along the way I built the tools the business runs on: an LLM tool that pulls the important parts out of pitch decks, <a href="/work/ai-reports">AI-drafted quarterly reports</a>, an <a href="/work/mcp-server">MCP server</a> that lets the team ask our data questions through Claude, and finally a <a href="/work/oneragtime-v2">rewrite of the whole platform</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Now</h2>
        <p>
          I build with coding agents every day, and I keep pushing how much of my work they can take on. On the side I'm building ThinClaw, an AI assistant with its own computer that runs a lot of my day-to-day. I'm looking for a senior backend
          or full-stack role. If that's what you're hiring for, <a href="/contact">say hi</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
<style>
  .page { background: var(--bg); color: var(--fg); padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 5rem) 5rem; }
  .title { font-size: var(--step-4); margin: 0 0 2rem; max-width: 16ch; }
  .top { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 320px; gap: 3rem; align-items: start; margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .portrait { width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 6px; }
  @media (max-width: 820px) { .top { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 1.5rem; } .portrait { max-width: 360px; } }
  .banner { margin: 0 0 1.75rem; }
  .banner :global(img) { width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 6px; display: block; }
  .banner figcaption { font-size: var(--step--1); color: var(--muted); margin-top: .5rem; }
  .side { float: right; width: 300px; margin: .25rem 0 1rem 2rem; }
  .side :global(img) { width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 6px; display: block; }
  .side figcaption { font-size: var(--step--1); color: var(--muted); margin-top: .5rem; }
  .prose h2 { clear: both; }
  @media (max-width: 820px) { .side { float: none; width: 100%; margin: 0 0 1.5rem; } }
</style>
